1. Key Concepts
• NGINX: An open-source, high-performance web server that serves as a reverse proxy, load balancer, and HTTP cache. It runs on an asynchronous, event-driven architecture designed to handle thousands of concurrent connections with low memory usage.
• GCP Compute Engine (GCE): Infrastructure-as-a-Service (IaaS) used to launch virtual machines (VMs) running Linux distributions (like Ubuntu or Debian) where NGINX is installed.
• Security Best Practice: The backend application runs strictly on an internal IP address. NGINX sits in the public subnet, processes incoming requests, filters out malicious behavior, and forwards clean requests to the backend.
2. Step-by-Step Deployment Guide
Step A: Provisioning the VM via Google Cloud Console
1. Go to the Google Cloud Console.
2. Navigate to Compute Engine ➔ VM Instances and click Create Instance.
3. Machine configuration: Choose a budget-friendly option for web hosting (e.g., e2-micro or e2-medium).
4. Boot Disk: Choose a Linux OS distribution (e.g., Ubuntu 22.04 LTS).
5. Networking & Firewall: Check the box to "Allow HTTP traffic" and "Allow HTTPS traffic". This creates automatic firewall rules to open ports 80 and 443.
6. Click Create.
Step B: Installation & Basic Setup
SSH into your newly created VM instance using the in-browser terminal or the gcloud SDK. Run the following commands:
bash
# Update the package repository index
sudo apt update -y

# Install the NGINX web server
sudo apt install nginx -y

# Verify NGINX is running active (running)
sudo systemctl status nginx
Use code with caution.
If your GCP Firewall rules are configured correctly, navigating to the instance's External IP address in a web browser will display the "Welcome to nginx" page.
3. Essential NGINX Configurations
NGINX operations are driven by directives housed in /etc/nginx/nginx.conf and modular server blocks located in /etc/nginx/sites-available/.
Reverse Proxy Block Configuration
To direct traffic from public port 80 to your internal application running on port 8080, edit your default configuration file (sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/default):
nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;
    server_name your_domain.com www.your_domain.com;

    location / {
        # Pass the incoming public traffic to the internal app
        proxy_pass http://localhost:8080/; 
        
        # Preserve original client headers for backend logging
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }

    # Serve static assets directly through NGINX for faster caching
    location /static/ {
        root /var/www/my-app/static;
    }
}
Use code with caution.
Apply and Test Configurations
Always validate your syntax before restarting the server to prevent unexpected site downtime:
bash
# Test NGINX configuration syntax
sudo nginx -t

# Reload configuration seamlessly without dropping active connections
sudo systemctl reload nginx
